[0] Okay, let's get started with today's lecture.
[1] Today we talk about the ⟦cash⟧ hierarchy in modern processors.
[2] A ⟦right⟧ ⟦back⟧ cache only writes a line to memory on eviction.
[3] Each cache line is 64 bytes on most x86 machines.
[4] So the the first question is how we find a line in the cache.
[5] We split the address into a tag, an index and an offset.
[6] The index selects a set, and the tag tells us whether we have a hit.
[7] With a direct mapped cache, every set has exactly one line.
[8] That gives us conflict misses when two addresses map to the same set.
[9] A ⟦two-way⟧ set associative cache halves the number of sets.
[10] The miss rate drops from 5 percent to about 3 percent.
